## 📐 Mathematical Formulations

### 1. Progressive Federal Income Tax

Federal income tax withholding is calculated across progressive income brackets after applying filing-status standard deductions ($S_d$):

$$\text{Taxable Income } (I) = \max(0, \text{Gross Pay} - S_d)$$

For $n$ tax brackets defined by lower bounds $B_i$, upper bounds $B_{i+1}$, and marginal rates $r_i$:

$$\text{Federal Tax} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \max\left(0, \min(I, B_{i+1}) - B_i\right) \times r_i$$

### 2. FICA Withholding & Statutory Caps (2026)

* **Social Security Tax:** $6.2\%$ applied up to the statutory wage cap of **$176,100**:
  $$\text{Social Security} = \min(\text{Gross Pay}, 176100) \times 0.062$$
* **Medicare Tax:** $1.45\%$ on all earnings + $0.9\%$ additional Medicare tax on individual earnings exceeding $200,000.

### 3. Net-to-Gross Reverse Calculation (Binary Search)

To calculate the required gross pay $G$ given a target net take-home pay $N_{\text{target}}$, the engine uses a monotonic binary search solver:

$$f(G) = \text{Net Pay}$$

Find $G \in [N_{\text{target}}, 2.5 \times N_{\text{target}}]$ such that:

$$\vert{}f(G) - N_{\text{target}}\vert{} \le 0.01$$

